Quoth Boudewijn Waijers in the annals of 
<cg35r0$aio$1@news3.tilbu1.nb.home.nl> ...

[alignment as an example of roleplaying or the lack thereof]
> 
> Others don't care at all about alignment, or break it when it's
> convenient only. Me for example, if I play a monk, I'll happily eat a
> floating eye and take the minor alignment hit. I'll kill with a samurai
> if I really need that peaceful dwarf's pick axe, but I won't poison a
> weapon.
> 
> Whatever I do in this regard, the game has a small alignment penalty, so
> I am already being punished for it. So the Dev Team obviously condones
> it, or they would have either made the penalty bigger or made it
> impossible at all.

It's at least arguable, though, that this is one example of the alignment 
system being broken. It's a simple subset of the fact that any role of 
any alignment can do virtually anything without worrying about alignment 
at all. About the only differences that anyone remarks on is what 
artifact weapons your god will give you and how many times the mysterious 
force knocks you back.

For those who don't like bothering to worry about alignment, the lack of 
differentiation is a plus. However, the devteam have said that one reason 
they're not interested in adding new roles and races is that "we're still 
trying to get the existing ones differentiated." Presumably, from their 
viewpoint, differentiation is a Good Thing. (And note, more 
differentiation between alignments would automatically cause more 
differentiation among roles and races, since most races can be of only 
one alignment and many roles are restricted from one or more alignments.)

Rob Ellwood (I think) once offered a suggestion for an alignment system 
with teeth. (Google for "an alignment system with teeth.") His solution 
was to express alignment as a ratio of good vs. bad alignment. Anything 
less than 50:1 would interfere with your god's goodwill toward you. To 
me, this fixes the results without solving the problem--that being that 
killing monsters automatically raises your alignment much faster than 
anything is likely to affect it negatively (hence the need for a ratio as 
large as 50:1). I'm not sure of the system I'd propose in its place: 
maybe having only conduct and not killing monsters affect alignment at 
all, maybe having killing monsters only have a small percentage chance of 
an alignment raise (and maybe tieing that to the difficulty level of the 
monster relative to the player character's level), maybe simply maxing 
out alignment at a low enough level that alignment hits would get you 
into negative territory no matter how many monsters you'd killed. The 
point is that I think it would be a good thing if players had to be 
genuinely concerned about actions that would negatively affect alignment.

The results of this would be that you would *have* to play differently 
aligned characters differently. Monks, Samurai, Knights, and Dwarves 
would become really different types of characters to play than 
Barbarians, Rogues, Elves, and Orcs. And I don't think all the difficulty 
should fall on the lawful characters. Some thought should be put into 
penalizing "lawful" behavior on the part of chaotics (a hit for paying a 
shopkeeper, perhaps? Or taming monsters rather than killing them--
possibly with the exception of domesticable animals?).
> 
> For example, for monks, they could have made them physically incapable
> of eating meat. They've been raised on a meatless diet, so their bowels
> aren't used to meat anymore. Let them throw up the moment they ingest
> anything fleshy. Since this is not implemented, we must assume that
> eating meat is only discouraged in a small way, but not forbidden.
> 
Yes, but that would be a rather ugly kludge, wouldn't it? Simply 
forbidding things within the game isn't generally The Nethack Way. Better 
to allow, but cause a trade-off worth thinking about.

About the "Monk conducts," I don't think they're all of the same type. 
Eating meat or wearing armor is one type--in-game messages tell you that 
this isn't "monklike" behavior. OTOH, weaponlessness appears to be purely 
optional. If you're not supposed to wield weapons, your god shouldn't 
give them to you as gifts.

